  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nRemaining quiet across the Nation's midsection this afternoon with \nmid-latitude flow focused across the far northern CONUS while a \nsubtropical ridge is noted over Florida and the Carolinas. In the \ncoming week, we'll see a variety of modest temperature swings with \nincreasing chances for rain. \n\nThis afternoon, relative humidity values have tumbled toward 35-45% \nthanks to deep mixing from aloft. A few wind gusts near 20 to 25 mph \nare sprouting up on surface observations. In light of the recent dry \nspell, these ingredients amount to a nonzero risk of fire weather \nconditions. \n\nA weak warm front along with increasing 850-mb flow will be the \nfocus for isolated showers or sprinkles (20-30% chance) on Friday, \nprimarily over the greater South Bend and Michiana area. Cross \nsections remain rather dry below 8,000 ft while the best low-level \nforcing is across Illinois. Dry conditions will prevail toward and \neast of the I-69 corridor. In the wake of the front, far southern \nareas will once again make a run for 80 degrees.\n\nOur primary rain chance this forecast period continues to be from \nany remnants of Hurricane Isaias, yet not everyone sees rain. Rain \nchances are greatest along and east of I-69 with rain amounts \ngenerally less then 0.5\" Saturday night through Sunday. Overall, no \nsignificant impacts expected from Isaias. It will be very warm \nSaturday (widespread low-80s) as a 582dm ridge builds over the \nMidwest. Not quite as warm to follow Sunday with clouds and cyclonic \nnortherly flow in play. \n\nA cold front is in the cards for the middle of next week, while the \nCONUS becomes dominated by a deep, cold trough descending over the \nnorthern Plains and a strong subtropical ridge to the southeast.",
